{
  "title": "ramekin-core: split ingredient_parser/item.rs and consolidate \
fraction knowledge",
  "description": "1. src/ingredient_parser/item.rs (1365 lines) bundles \
entity/unicode normalization (74-186), prep-note detection (189-530, ~10 \
functions), yield/equipment/label line classifiers (532-760), and title-casing \
(770-930). Split into normalize.rs / prep_notes.rs / line_classifiers.rs. \
Similarly, extract/html_fallback.rs mixes Jetpack, WPRM, Dotdash-Meredith, and \
old-WordPress-div strategies in one file — split per CMS family.\n\
\n\
2. Fraction/amount knowledge is scattered: unicode->ASCII fraction mapping in \
item.rs:108-140; ASCII fraction->decimal in amounts.rs:21-89; a third \
fraction-aware regex character class embedded in blog.rs:713 and \
extract/mod.rs:34; the '1/2 -> 0.5' doc-comment block copy-pasted at \
amounts.rs:18-20 and :57-60. Consolidate 'what counts as a fraction' \
(is_fraction, the unicode map, the inline regexes) so the extract layer \
references the parser's definition instead of re-enumerating glyphs — reduces \
the risk of extractor and parser disagreeing about what a quantity looks \
like.\n\
\n\
Intended behavior-preserving, but touches parsing: rerun `make pipeline` and \
commit data/ diffs; unintended diffs mean behavior changed.",
